  'Predicts which memory the CPU will need next and loads it into cache early. The effect depends on the workload. It helps code that reads memory in order: loading, decompression, encoding, most apps. It can hurt code with random access patterns, such as many game engines and databases, and all-core work that already saturates memory bandwidth. In those cases wrong guesses evict useful data and waste bandwidth. Users have reported big gains in 1% lows in some games with it off, e.g. about +20% in Overwatch on a 9900K. Results differ by CPU and game, so benchmark it rather than assume.',
